after four generations in more than 5.4  million units sold the new say at Ibiza  has been thoroughly revamped but that  doesn't absolutely have to mean improved  let's have a look at what the Spanish  car makers come up with the car tester  Emmanuelle Shaffer knows the CID beats  has always been a practical everyday car  partly because it's so compact the fifth  generations grown a bit larger though  now he'll see if it's got what it takes  to carry on the Ibiza success story at  three point six five metres long and one  point six wide the first he beats in  1984 was noticeably smaller than this  one which adds nearly 40 centimetres of  length and about 20 centimetres of width  but the engines have barely changed in  size the first generation ranging from  0.9 to 1.7 litres in the current one  from one to one-and-a-half the  performance and consumption on the other  hand have been greatly improved more on  that later the new e beats is built on  Volkswagens nqb a zero platform but the  design language is a closed dialect of  the one spoken by big brother Leon  sharply defined contours and tapered  forms predominate  [Music]  unlike previous generations this Ibiza  is available only in a five-door version  sculptured ribs and lines run along the  entire length the rear gives a tight  crisp impression with a hint of the  first generations jutting taillights  position to the outside Emanuel is quite  pleased with the interior the occupants  benefit from the greater width and it  has more space in general so passenger  and Driver don't end up climbing all  over each other  emmanuel likes the accents with colors  matching the exterior paint he also  thinks the way the buttons for the  infotainment system are integrated into  the glass surface look smart and they're  easy to operate the platform allows for  the ibiza to be fitted with technical  features and assistance systems  previously seen only in the upper  vehicle range like the SE at Lyon or the  Atika such as for instance the radar  obstacle scanning prenta cyst with city  emergency braking the adaptive cruise  control and state-of-the-art parking  sensors with a rear-view camera  take off along the immanuel points out  that the he beats his cargo space holds  from 355 to 1165 liters so even with the  rear seat up you've still got room for a  large suitcase and several smaller bags  a line of six inches are available for  the Ibiza the 1 liter TSI and the car we  tested can reach a top speed of 195 km/h  average consumption is factory rated at  four point seven liters per 100  kilometers  yeah the manual thinks the 1 liter TSI  is output of 85 kilowatts is ideal for a  car like this not too much and not too  little  it's zippy and accelerates nicely out of  the curves  it's very nimble while keeping fuel  consumption at a moderate level of all  the first generation he beats a  tormented potential buyers with an  intimidating choice of 14 trim levels  the current generation has cut that down  to a soothingly manageable 5 saving time  and stress no animal sees the new Ibiza  with its larger dimensions an additional  space as a good vehicle for everyday  giving around with 2 or 4 occupants with  the rear seat up or down it's all no  problem of course it won't fit into the  really tight parking spots anymore like  its predecessors did but it's a good car  for getting around town all finished up  the new Ibiza starts at 12,000 490 euros  in Germany a bigger engine and some  options can swiftly push that total up  beyond 25 thousand euros
 

